{
  "dom_id": "step-t4833",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an even exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^6 = 64?\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $6$th root of each side and solving for $x.$ \n\nSince the exponent $(6)$ is even, there will be two real solutions (positive and negative):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{{x}^{6}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{{2}^{6}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n*When $n$ is even, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a$ with $a \\geq 0,$ we get two solutions (positive and negative):* \n\n$$\nx = \\pm\\sqrt[n]{a}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If $a<0$ and $n$ is even, equation  $x^n = a$ has no solution since, in this case, $x^n \\geq 0$ for any real $x.$",
